A||||30||Spektrum-87GT||getting rid of the egr||||1||
Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||09-27-2001||10:21 PM||||i've read on some engines you can block off egr valves/tubes... i've got the 2.8 and live in a place with no emmision laws. I'm also having a problem with a jumpy idle and inconsistant rpms and ive been told vacuum leaks in the egr is probably the problem. anyone ever plugged up the egr system? what effects does it have?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||09-27-2001||11:01 PM||||Why not just fix the problem by fixing rather than removing?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spektrum-87GT||09-27-2001||11:18 PM||||well... from my understanding, it doesnt add to performance and is there mainly for emissions. egr parts are fairly expensive... and if i dont need it, forget it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Banzai||09-27-2001||11:37 PM||||As you may or may not know, the EGR introduces already spent gasses into the combustion chamber, thereby providing less total oxygen content in the cylinder. More oxygen means more power(with added fuel) right? We all know that. BUT, the key reason for the EGR is that the intorduction of a burnt mixture also serves to COOL the combustion chamber. Lowering hydrocarbon emmisons.  If you have a motor that is DESIGNED to run with an EGR, it's components (i.e. valves, etc.) are designed to run at certain combustion chamber temps.  I've rebuilt many modern EGR equipped motors. The engineers spend a great deal of time integrating the EGR system into the engine. As evidenced by the passages that are cast into the intakes and cylinder heads. The point? Fix the problem. Check your EGR as described in the manuals, if it's bad, replace it.  Replace all your vaccum lines. If your in a wrench'n mood buy a new intake gasket set, and while your in there, clean out those EGR passageways.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||09-27-2001||11:53 PM||||I have mine disconnected and have had for some time. I know some will disagree with me but I promise you it will run better. As far as you erratic idle the EGR valve does not operate at idle speed, so you may have a vacuum leak somewhere else. I removed mine because I had a pulse speed around thirty five MPH.( sort of hunt and search) also I still run 87 octang and no pinging. also mine is a 86 and my son has a mint 88gt but mine will get him on the quarter mile. I also have the MSD coil which I like very much. I am not a racer I just like a car when I kick it I want it to go.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SLYGUY||09-28-2001||01:49 AM||||From what I've been told, the EGR is essential to the system. The ECU is looking for it and will not monitor the engine or configure the mixture/timing/spark, etc without it. <P>My manuals confirmed what I've been told about the EGR. It does indeed assist the idle. My 88GT had a crappy idle. Sometimes it would lope up to +-400rpm.<P>New EGR fixed all that. Smooter on startup. No lope. Car runs better with it.<P>Besides, you run the chance of burning a piston without it. Fix it and RUN it.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe Torma||09-28-2001||02:01 AM||||I agree, if the computer is programmed to use it, it should need to be in working order.  If not you may experience pinging.<P>Though...I questioned the makers of my TEC-II engine control system if I need to use the EGR and they said no.  They said to give the system adequate fuel and timing and watch the knock sensor(and adjust).  Note this is for my 3400.<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">   <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 09-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||09-28-2001||08:58 AM||||Plugging the vacum line to the EGR will stop it from operating.  No engine is designed to run with EGR.  EGR is purely an emmisions thing and does exactly described above.  It will not effect your heads or make your engine run hotter.  This is a myth.  The same myth that says without the Cat you will lose bottom end torque.  While that may be slightly true as I said before muscle car owners never said "Damn I wish I had something to clog my exhaust so I would get more torque.  Likewise the same metal used in engines then is used now.  They dont temper it to work at lower temps.  You could pull alll of the computer controlled crap out of the fiero and run a carb with no emmisons equipment on the 2.8 and it would run fine.  The O2 sensor will not even throw a code with the EGR disconnected BTW. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||09-28-2001||09:46 AM||||Falcon, you're oversimplifying.  <P>The Fiero is tuned to work the the EGR, so by removing it, the ECM is no longer getting the exhaust gas it expects, but since the ECM doesn't know this, it can't adjust for it, and there's your problem.  If you change the air/fuel mixture, you have to retune the engine to make it run optimally.  It's just like adding headers on an old pre-emissions engine.  The lower backpressure would cause your carb jetting to be thrown off, and you'd have to re-jet.<P>As for the "myth" of the converters, well, dyno's don't speak in mythological terms.  It's a proven fact that some backpressure (to a point) will improve your low end torque, but it does this at the expense of high rpm horsepower.  I could tell this when I took my 69 Firebird and removed the single exhaust and put duals on.  It felt a bit weaker off the line, but came alive at higher rpms.  It's always a trade off when tuning a car.||||reg||13
Z||000009||Raydar||09-28-2001||11:25 AM||||Ed Parks said that he has seen more than a few 2.8s with the same two burned pistons, that had the EGR disconnected. That's good enough for me.<BR>Of course, it could be a coincidence. YMMV.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||2xsess||09-28-2001||11:30 AM||||the ECM is programmed to loko for it, Joe Wyman makes new chips that remove this and it prevents the burnt pistons, that's what I've been told. With the EGR just unhooked the fuel air mix is too rich or soemthing like that. Ask Trav (1FST2M6), he told me about it but I forgot.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">           <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||09-28-2001||12:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2xsess:<BR><B>the ECM is programmed to loko for it, Joe Wyman makes new chips that remove this and it prevents the burnt pistons, that's what I've been told. With the EGR just unhooked the fuel air mix is too rich or soemthing like that. Ask Trav (1FST2M6), he told me about it but I forgot.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The EGR just introduces an inert gas (exhaust) to the mixture. It doesn't lean it or richen it. Just stops it from burning so hot.<BR>I'd be curious to see if an engine with Joe's chip and disabled EGR would still pass an emissions sniffer test. (Atlanta is a PITA)<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||09-28-2001||12:07 PM||||With the EGR removed the car runs lean. Which does cause a slight rise in head temps and can cause burnt pistons. The fuel/air curve needs to be adjusted. An easy way is different injectors and an adjustable fuel pressure regulator. The O2 sensor will tell the computer what the air/fuel ratio is. If you advance your timing to 12 and re-adjust the fuel you will be find the car runs cooler. Just disconnecting the EGR won't solve your idle problem. There is a vacuum leak somewhere that needs to be fixed. The computer only checks the EGR solenoid. The EGR itself can be removed and the computer won't know it as long as the solenoid works.<BR>As for "performance" gains, it is very minimal. Once adjusted correctly the engine will run slightly cooler and get better gas mileage. It may knock .01 seconds off your 1/4 mile time.||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||09-28-2001||01:53 PM||||Orief, That was what I meant to say thanks for clarifying what I thought in my head.  What I wanted to say was that the ECM only knows about the the solenoid.  BTW I know some back pressure is good.  Most mufflers supply enough backpressure.  What I meant by this is that everyone hears things and takes their effect to far.  Just so you know I am aware of how jumbleb my thoughts are as i have been awake for 48 hrs straight! ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Voytek||09-28-2001||02:45 PM||||Hey Oreif - now that you mention it, let me change the subject just slightly.  Does that mean that a faulty EGR could make the car run hotter?  I do get the check engine light at higher speeds quite often (I'm quite positive it's the EGR) and my car tends to run fairly hot most of the time.  Any connection?<BR>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||falconhulk||09-28-2001||02:58 PM||||I have been running with the egr vacum line plugged for weeks and I dont run a degree hotter.  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||NEPTUNE||09-28-2001||03:45 PM||||heres' 2 cents more... sounds to me like you have a rusted/cracked egr tube. the valve itself is very durable, mine has nearly 130000 miles on it. Also without the egr, you may get some detonation(pinging) if you run regulae gas. LOL.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Brandon86SE||09-28-2001||03:58 PM||||Alright, I don't claim to be an expert on the subject, but I can tell you my story!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I bought my car 2 years ago, and really didn't know much about cars then.  Over these couple years I have become a bit of a "backyard mechanic".  The first time I knew that my EGR was plugged was when Tony (Calgary Fiero Owner's Group founder) was taking a look at my car on the day of the first meeting I attended.  He pointed it out to me.  I have been driving the car for 2 years, and put on 30,000 km's (From 63,000 - 93,000) with it this way.  Plus there's however many km's on the car with the EGR plugged from before I bought it.  I run regular gas.  I have no pinging, I don't run hot, and I've never had any problems with the engine.  I don't claim to know so, but I've read that removing the EGR does add hp.  This makes sense to me, since it is blowing exhaust back into the engine....  Anyway, I just wanted to offer my experience as a case of someone running without EGR, and doing just fine!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000018||88formula||09-28-2001||04:06 PM||||The sole purpose of the EGR system is to lower NOX emissions by introducing an inert gas into the intake charge. NOX emissions are caused by high combustion temperatures because today’s engines run with leaner mixtures for reduced emissions and fuel economy. High combustion temperatures cause the fuel to reach it's flash point before the spark plug ignites the mixture causing pre-ignition and detonation. Pre-ignition and detonation will ruin any piston even a forged piston. It brakes ring lands, melts pistons, and burns holes in pistons. It can even burn head gaskets. <P>In the good old muscle car days before emissions laws engines would run with rich fuel mixtures thereby lowering the combustion temperatures and they had leaded gasoline also, which has a high octane tolerance.<P>You may not see you actual engine temperatures on your pod gauge go up and you may not here the detonation. If you disconnect your EGR valve without changing anything else you run the risk of ruining your engine.<P>The EGR valve only operates at part throttle conditions. It does not operate at full throttle and it does not operate at idle or when the engine is cold. If you’re racing at the track disconnecting the EGR will do nothing because the computer disables it at full throttle anyway.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||falconhulk||09-28-2001||04:16 PM||||Hey heres a question for ya.....does the fiero have a nock sensor?  I am not sure..................||||reg||1
Z||000020||Cooter||09-28-2001||07:35 PM||||Mine does  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||BV MotorSports||09-28-2001||10:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Banzai:<BR><B>If you have a motor that is DESIGNED to run with an EGR, it's components (i.e. valves, etc.) are designed to run at certain combustion chamber temps.  I've rebuilt many modern EGR equipped motors. The engineers spend a great deal of time integrating the EGR system into the engine. As evidenced by the passages that are cast into the intakes and cylinder heads. <BR>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I understand where you are coming from but, removing the EGR will NOT hurt your engine or performance at all. I have a 94 Formula 6 speed. When I wanted to remove the EGR, I went to the local GM dealer and picked up the EGR block off plate that is installed on ALL LT1's (and LS1's)shipped outside of the US. There is nothing different between the US and Non US market engine w/ or w/o the EGR besides the block off plate. <P>Steven<P><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000022||Spektrum-87GT||09-29-2001||12:04 AM||||im getting a lot of mixed opinion(ok, some are facts) but it seems that the engine runs lean already... and without the recirculating exhaust, it runs even leaner... so without a reprogrammed computer, dont plug the egr or you'll turn your pistons into toast metal. one thing i dont understand... doesnt the computer sense the oxygen amount and automatically adjust the amount of fuel? i dont know much about how the fieros computer/fuel injection works, but wouldnt the computer automatically give it more fuel if it sensed more oxygen or a lean fuel mixture?||||reg||1
Z||000023||Oreif||09-29-2001||12:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B>im getting a lot of mixed opinion(ok, some are facts) but it seems that the engine runs lean already... and without the recirculating exhaust, it runs even leaner... so without a reprogrammed computer, dont plug the egr or you'll turn your pistons into toast metal. one thing i dont understand... doesnt the computer sense the oxygen amount and automatically adjust the amount of fuel? i dont know much about how the fieros computer/fuel injection works, but wouldnt the computer automatically give it more fuel if it sensed more oxygen or a lean fuel mixture?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There is a limit as to how much fuel the computer can add. It is limited by the fuel pressure and flow rate of the injectors. At full throttle the EGR is closed so it doesn't take effect. The problem is "city" driving that you'll get the lean condition and possible pinging.<P>Voytek, If you have a small vacuum leak in the EGR system it could be causing an excessive lean condition. Most folks here decribe it as "glowing manifolds" The check engine light at high speeds is probably being set by the O2 sensor. Since the computer expects the EGR to be closed and the O2 is still reading a high lean condition it could exceed the limits of the O2 and cause the check engine light to come on. When the light comes on, put the car in neutral (or push clutch in) and rev the motor to 4000 and let off the gas. If the light goes off when it gets below 2000 rpm you are running too lean. (High rpm with a quick fall off and normal exhaust back pressure will create a slightly rich condition) The cause for the lean condition can be many things but I would look for vacuum leaks first.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Cooter||09-29-2001||12:27 AM||||I am in the most basic stages of learning how to tune ECMs, so I am probably going out on a limb here-<BR> In the Fiero prom, when the ecm calls for the EGR function, it adds timing to offset the slower burn of the diluted incoming air mixture. Without a knock sensor, the ecm doesn't hear then spark knock from the added timing during egr operation, so you might get some weird performance. Individual results may vary.||||reg||1
Z||000025||ka4nkf||09-30-2001||12:39 AM||||Let me say one more thing. If some of you older fellows like me, you will remember that when you went to buy a used car that you would always look in the end of the exhaust pipe and see if it was black or white. If it was white you were satisfied. but if it was black you laid off. Now if that tail-pipe is white then that engine is burning every drop of gas it gets and making horsepower. When yhis pipe is white this is known to be carbon-monoxide, which the govt. does not want and I agree with them. So the auto-maker introduces EGR into the combustion chamber to weaken the fuel burning which causes less carbon-monoxide. And this is good for our country for pollution. But don't tell me that it will not have more power without it.Just take a look at all of the new cars and see if you can find a clean tail-pipe. I have seen some but not many.||||reg||1
Z||000026||johnt671||09-30-2001||09:45 AM||||Something to check if you have EGR problems. I recently changed my throttle body and when I removed the old one I found the passages under the gasket were completely plugged up with carbon.  You could have a perfectly functioning egr valve but the blocked passages make it ineffective.||||reg||1
Z||000027||falconhulk||10-01-2001||08:29 AM||||BV MotorSports<BR>That post is very interesting!  This would seem to prove burnt heads are impossible by blocking off the egr.  I was starting to change my opinion...hey everybody is wrong sometimes but you post brought me to my senses.||||reg||1
Z||000028||BurlyGoat||10-01-2001||12:25 PM||||I am also looking at removing my EGR (not for better performance just cause I'm to cheap to buy a $80 tube). Would I be correct to say, Block off the EGR and if you notice any of the symptoms (i.e. Pinging, red manifolds, etc.), just put the EGR back on ? That way if I pay attention to the car I shouldn't hurt it.||||reg||1
Z||000029||falconhulk||10-01-2001||12:49 PM||||I use premium octane anyway since my timing is advanced.  I am not sure if the Fiero has a knock sensor as I have found no documentation on it, or its location.  I would think it had one but hey maybe not.  Why would they sell an EGR blockoff plate if it damaged the engines?  Oreif<BR>is a very smart guy and I have read many great posts by him.  What bothers me is that my own general knowledge disagrees with his Fiero specific knowledge.  Has anyone on here personally had burnt cylinder heads as a result of disconnecting the EGR?  That you can say without a doubt the lack of EGR caused it?  I think Oreif's explanation makes complete sense.  But I also think most of the people on the other side make a lot of sense.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Tigger||10-01-2001||12:52 PM||||I got curious one day dinkering & cleaning my 2.5L and found a set screw blocking off the vacuum line to the EGR.<P>I removed the set screw, and it ran like crap and idled real high.<P>Then I immediately remembered I had disconnected the IAC and forgot to plug it back in.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Hooked the IAC back up and dang if didn't run the best it's ever ran with the EGR system back in line.<P>My mileage went up, feels like it has better performance, runs better and smoother with the EGR.||||reg||1
